#. §6: `exist`
#. §7: `exist`
#. §8: ```
#. exist a
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.L03_quant
msgid "## **Level 3**\n"
"This valid argument is sometimes called **Existential Generalization**. It says that if you know that a predicate (like §0) is true for some §1 of type §2, then we can deduce that there exists an element §3 of type §4 such that §5 is true. In order to prove this valid argument, we need a way to reckon with an existential goal in Lean. We need a new tactic ...\n"
"### **❯ The §6 tactic**\n"
"The most direct way to prove an existential goal is to supply a **witness** to the statement. The §7 tactic is handy in this regard. Specifically, 👉 try:\n"
"§8"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P`
#. §1: `R`
#. §2: `Q`
#. §3: `R`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L01_equiv
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The result of this level tells you that if you want to prove and if-then statement where the hypothesis is a disjunction, then you may do so by... **assuming** that §0 is true and showing that §1 must follow, ***and*** as a separate goal assuming that §2 is true and showing that §3 must following in this case as well."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `apply double_inclusion`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples.cartprod_example1
msgid "Double inclusion exercise involving Cartesian products.\n"
"\n"
"Note: It is possible to prove this just using rewrites, but the intent is to get double inclusion practice. Start with §0 to set up your double inclusion proof."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ P ∧ ¬ P ↔ F₀ $$
#. §1: $F₀$
#. §2: $$
#. \\begin{array}{|cc||c|}
#.   \\hline
#.   P & ¬ P & P ∧ ¬ P \\
#.   \\hline
#.   T & F & F \\
#.   F & T & F \\
#.   \\hline
#. \\end{array}
#. $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.equiv_eg_docs
msgid "This is an **Inverse Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"\n"
"where §1 is a contradiction. We can prove the **Inverse Law** with a truth-table\n"
"§2"
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ P ∨ F₀ ↔ P $$
#. §1: $F₀$
#. §2: ```
#. Or_false (P : Prop) : P ∨ False ↔ P
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg.arg_eg_docs
msgid "This is an **Identity Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"\n"
"where §1 is a contradiction. Here's what it looks like in Lean:\n"
"§2"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `NAND`
#. §1: `⊼`
#. §2: ```
#. nand_def (P Q : Prop) : P ⊼ Q ↔ ¬ (P ∧ Q)
#. ```
#. §3: `⊼`
#. §4: `\\barwedge`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L06_equiv
msgid "# **Level 6: Not and Nand**\n"
"We introduce a (seemingly) new connective in this level: the §0 connective (not-and) denoted §1. Here's how it is defined in Lean:\n"
"§2\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting-tip**\n"
"In order to typeset the symbol §3 type §4."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Not_not`
#. §1: `Not_not`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "Here's where our newly unlocked theorem §0 can be handy. 🔍 Check out the entry for §1 in the **Theorems** tab."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∨`
#. §1: `⊼`
#. §2: `¬`
#. §3: `∨`
#. §4: `∧`
#. §5: `⊼`
#. §6: `True`
#. §7: `False`
#. §8: `⊼`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L08_equiv
msgid "### **💡 All the food for thought**\n"
"The binary operator §0 can be expressed in terms of §1. The last three examples show that the unary operator §2 and the binary operators §3 and §4 can be expressed in terms of §5. In fact, *any* binary operator on §6 and §7 can be expressed in terms of §8!"
msgstr ""

